.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt VIRTIO_BLK 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm virtio_blk
.Nd virtio block device driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/diskN
.Pp
.Pa /dev/diskNpM
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver drives each virtio block device of the virtio-pci transport, up to
eight, as the disk a virtual machine is given with
.Ql -device virtio-blk-pci .
Each becomes a disk with the nodes
.Xr disk 4
describes.
The kernel probes AHCI, then NVMe, then virtio-blk, then ATA, and the first
disk found is disk 0.
Partitions, mounts, a root selected by
.Ql rach2.root=
and a boot partition selected by
.Ql rach2.boot=
take a virtio disk as they take a SATA disk.
.Pp
.Pa /proc/disk
lists the disk with transport
.Ql virtio
and a location of its place among the virtio block devices found.
Its serial number is what the device answers to
.Dv VIRTIO_BLK_T_GET_ID ,
in
.Pa /sys/block/diskN/device/serial ;
it has no model or firmware revision, as on Linux.
.Pa /sys/bus/virtio/devices/virtioN
shows the device bound to
.Ql virtio_blk ,
with the feature bits taken.
.Pp
The driver makes one virtqueue for each processor, up to as many as the
device offers and eight at most, each with indirect descriptor tables, so
that a request of up to 1 MiB is one command.
A request goes on the queue of the processor that makes it.
The device's block size must be 512 bytes.
A device that offers
.Dv VIRTIO_BLK_F_RO
is read-only.
.Pp
The boot reads and writes by polling the queues.
Once the root is mounted, each device is moved to its interrupts, an
MSI-X message for each queue where the transport has one; a transfer then
sleeps its caller until its queue's message, and a page fault with
interrupts off still polls.
.Pa /proc/interrupts
lists each vector as
.Ql virtioN .
The device keeps polling when the kernel command line has
.Ql rach2.vblkpoll ,
and when the transport could give it no interrupt.
.Pp
Virtio has no force unit access.
When the device has a write cache, which it reports with
.Dv VIRTIO_BLK_F_FLUSH
and its writeback setting, a write is followed by a
.Dv VIRTIO_BLK_T_FLUSH
command before it returns, so it is on the medium when it returns.
A file system that writes several blocks and flushes once after them
sends them without the flush, and its flush is the device's.
.Pp
.Dv BLKDISCARD
and
.Dv BLKZEROOUT ,
which
.Xr disk 4
describes, become
.Dv VIRTIO_BLK_T_DISCARD
and
.Dv VIRTIO_BLK_T_WRITE_ZEROES
when the device offers them, in pieces of at most the sectors its
configuration allows;
.Pa /sys/block/diskN/queue/discard_max_bytes
gives that size.
A device without write zeroes has the zeros written as data.
.Pp
When the device's configuration changes, the next transfer reads its
capacity again.
When the transport lets the device go, as a write of its function to
.Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/virtio-pci/unbind
does, the disk goes out of use as a stick that is pulled: its nodes go,
its mounts read and write nothing more, and every transfer in flight fails
with
.Er EIO .
.Pp
The metal kernel's read-only policy, which
.Xr disk 7
describes, judges a virtio command by the ATA command it corresponds to: a
read and
.Dv GET_ID
are allowed, and a write, a flush, a discard and a write of zeros are
refused.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The driver writes these lines to the kernel log, with
.Ar virtioN
the device's name on the virtio bus.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: S MiB, Q queues, P pieces of B bytes, K sectors a request, cache on|off ...
The device was brought up, with what it offers among read-only, discard
and write zeroes.
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: refused: blocks of B bytes; the block layer counts in 512-byte sectors
The device's block size is not 512 bytes; it is not published.
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: unusable: reason
The device refused the features, its queues or DRIVER_OK, or did not
answer
.Dv GET_ID ;
it is reset and the boot goes on.
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: request type T failed: status S
The device failed the command, and the transfer fails with
.Er EIO ,
or with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
when the device does not support the command.
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: request type T failed: no answer in thirty seconds
The device did not answer a polled command within the wait, five seconds
with interrupts off.
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: out of use: reason
The device did not answer, its queue had no room for the wait, or it was
removed.
It is reset, or kept from reaching memory when the reset does not end,
every transfer it held fails with
.Er EIO ,
and so does every later one.
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: capacity: S MiB, was R MiB
The device's capacity changed.
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: interrupts: Q queues
The device was moved to its queues' interrupts.
.It Ql virtio-blk: virtioN: polled: reason
The device keeps polling, for the reason given.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr disk 4 ,
.Xr nvme 4 ,
.Xr virtio-gpu 4 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr sysfs 5 ,
.Xr disk 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The device, its features and its requests are those of section 5.2 of
the Virtual I/O Device (VIRTIO) specification, version 1.2.
.Sh CAVEATS
Linux names a virtio disk
.Pa /dev/vdX
and its partitions
.Pa /dev/vdXN ;
Rach2 names it as any other disk.
Linux waits for a device's answer without bound; Rach2 gives a device
thirty seconds, since a page fault may be waiting on it with interrupts
off, and then takes it out of use.
Rach2 does not take the zoned, secure-erase or lifetime commands, packed
virtqueues, or queues that poll beside those that interrupt.
